step of clustering. First, we group 864 formations into 18 clusters where 18 is the minimum number of clusters that main formations except 4-4-2 and 4-2-3-1 are separated from one another. At the next step, the cluster in which 4-4-2 and 4-2-3-1 coexist (i.e., the mixture of Fig. 3(c) and Fig. 3(d) ) is further split into 18 sub-clusters, with one indicating 4-4-2 and all the others indicating 4-2-3-1. After merging clusters with less than 15 observations into “others” group and those indicating 4-2-3-1 into one group, we get seven formation groups as illustrated in Fig. 3 . A.3. Python Implementation We have implemented the role representation algorithm using Python 3.8 on our own, while adopted the R package gSeg 2 2 2 https://rdrr.io/cran/gSeg/ developed by Chen et al. ( Chen and Zhang 2015 ) , Chu et al. ( Chu and Chen 2019 ) , and Song et al. ( Song and Chen 2020 ) for discrete g-segmentation. To perform the whole process at once, we utilize the Python package rpy2 3 3 3 https://rpy2.github.io to run the R script with gSeg inside our Python implementation. The process was executed on an Intel Core i7-8550U CPU with 16.0GB of memory. (Note that the M1 chip by Apple does not support binaries for rpy2 ’s API mode in Python, raising a memory error.) Our implementation is publicly accessible in a GitHub repository named soccercpd 4 4 4 https://github.com/pientist/soccercpd with sample GPS data from an anonymized match. Figure 10 . A snapshot of the Tableau animation of SoccerCPD. Appendix B Comparison Against Baselines To empirically justify our model selection, we have performed an additional experiment by comparing the results in Section 6.1 against those using other baseline CPD methods. Since FormCPD and RoleCPD handle different types of sequences, the sets of compatible baselines also differ from one another. FormCPD takes discrete high-dimensional data ( 10 × 10 10	imes 10 binary matrices) where parametric methods assuming a certain distribution on a Euclidean space show poor performances. Thus, we adopted nonparametric methods including kernel methods ( Shawe-Taylor and Cristianini 2004 ) and rank-based methods ( Lung-Yut-Fong et al. 2015 ) . We also estimated a formation per session without finding a change-point as a naive baseline. For RoleCPD, the observations are permutations in a symmetric group where we cannot even define an inner product or an order. This means that kernel methods and rank-based methods are not applicable as well as the other off-the-shelf CPD methods for ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} . Hence, we have only used the 5-minute smoothing to find instructed roles per time window as in Bialkowski et al. ( Bialkowski et al. 2014c ) . To implement the CPD baselines, we utilize the Python package ruptures 5 5 5 https://centre-borelli.github.io/ruptures-docs/ developed by Truong et al. ( Truong et al. 2020 ) . The applied methods are listed below. • Avg. g-seg. (for both): Discrete g-segmentation using the average scan statistic. ( gseg1_discrete in gSeg ) • Union g-seg. (for both): Discrete g-segmentation using the union scan statistic ( gseg1_discrete in gSeg ). • RBF kernel (only for FormCPD): Kernel CPD with the radial basis function kernel ( CostRbf in ruptures ). • Cosine kernel (only for FormCPD): Kernel CPD with the cosine similarity kernel ( CostCosine in ruptures ). • Rank (only for FormCPD): CPD using a rank transformation ( CostRank in ruptures ). • None (only for FormCPD): Estimating a formation from the entire session without applying a CPD method. • 5-min seg. (only for RoleCPD): Segmenting a player period (obtained by splitting a session by player substitutions) into 5-minute windows and find the most frequent roles for each player per window. Table 2 shows the results of SoccerCPD for each method. All the baselines except for the non-CPD one split the total of 807 sessions into about 870 formation periods and 2,200 role periods. It clearly demonstrates that higher performances can be obtained when employing CPD methods instead of the naive segmentation. Moreover, discrete g-segmentation with the average scan statistic outperforms the other CPD methods. Appendix C Tableau Visualization Lastly, we visualize our results as an animation using Tableau 2020.4. Fig. 10 is a snapshot of the generated animation. It dynamically shows all the following tactical information in one dashboard: • Player trajectories colored by temporary roles in the right. • Player-to-temporary-role assignments as colored bar codes in the left timeline, divided into detected role periods. • Formation per role period by the mean role locations in the bottom with the emphasis on the current formation. • Player-to-instructed-role assignments per role period by annotating uniform numbers below the role circles. • Switching players indicated by inflating the corresponding circles both in the formations and the pitch image. We have published the full-version video on YouTube 6 6 6 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F-tvG1-MLps . We expect that this visual interface effectively supports domain participants to review and analyze the match by tracking tactical changes and discovering switching patterns. ◄ Feeling lucky? Conversion report Report an issue View&nbsp;original on&nbsp;arXiv ► Copyright Privacy Policy Generated by L a T e XML oxide

